# Module optimize — systematic branch cleanup

Whenever you finish updating code on the current branch, run a **single coherent optimization pass** over everything the branch touches. Do not leave scattered one-off fixes, duplicate helpers, or half-finished refactors.

## Before optimizing

Materialise the full branch scope (same base as `module-changelog.mdc`: prefer `origin/master`, or `origin/main` if `origin/master` does not exist):

```powershell
# Prefer origin/master; use origin/main if origin/master does not exist
git fetch origin master
git diff --stat origin/master...HEAD
git diff origin/master...HEAD
```

If `origin/master` does not exist, run the same commands against `origin/main` instead.

Work from the diff — not from memory.

## Optimization pass (one pass, not drive-by edits)

1. **Group by concern** — schema, config, API wrapper, admin UI, cron, etc. Each concern should have one clear entry point; merge overlapping changes into the same class/file when they belong together.
2. **Deduplicate** — collapse copy-pasted blocks into existing helpers; reuse module utilities (`Db` wrappers, loggers, constants) instead of new parallel implementations.
3. **Remove dead code** — delete unused methods, variables, imports, config keys, SQL fragments, templates, and commented-out blocks introduced or left obsolete by this branch.
4. **Align with neighbours** — naming, error handling, and SQL discipline must match the surrounding file and module (see `module-sql.mdc`, `module-logging.mdc`, `module-php.mdc`, `module-prestashop.mdc`).
5. **Keep scope honest** — optimize what this branch changed or made redundant; do not unrelated-refactor files outside the branch diff unless the user asked.

## What "systematic" means

- ✅ One review of the branch diff → a short list of consolidation/deletion tasks → apply them together.
- ❌ Tweaking one file now and promising to "clean up later" in another file.
- ❌ Adding a second helper when an equivalent already exists in the module.
- ❌ Leaving deprecated code paths "just in case" when the branch fully replaced them.

## When done

Re-run `git diff --stat <base>...HEAD` using the same base branch and confirm no redundant files or duplicate logic remain in the changed set.
